Well, here it is, the product of ages of grueling work, slaving at my
PC for at least 2 or 3 hours total (or maybe more!):   :-)

The NetHack 3.1 Kill List patch!

Yes, that's right: Freshly ported from NH 3.0 to NH 3.1!

HANS: Find out just what kind of adventurer you really are!  

FRANS: Yah! Are you a He-Man --

HANS: Or a Girrrly-Man?

:-)

Uh, thanks, Hans & Frans.  Now anyway, adding a kill list to your copy of 
NetHack 3.1 should be pretty easy.  You need only modify one .c file 
(end.c), and no .h files (if you don't want to, anyway).

1) Make a backup copy of end.c (just in case...)

2) For the next three snippets of code (separated by comments), locate the
"quoted" part in end.c (with the >'s), and add the unquoted code 
immediately after it.  (No, don't add the >'s into end.c!)  :-)

3) Next, place the large hunk of code/comments at the bottom of this file 
at the bottom of end.c.

4) Compile and enjoy!

Tech notes:
a) DISCLAIMER (For the programming impaired ;-) ): You need to be able to 
compile NH 3.1 source for this to work!  I know of no easy way to "patch the 
binaries" to add it to an already compiled verson.

b) I am running under OS/2 TTY, and the windowing calls to display the list
work fine (I mimicked the calls from enlightenment().), but I can't 
100% guarantee that this will be correct on a real windowing setup, since
I don't know if there are any little quirks or whatever on some systems that 
I might have missed.  Anyway, I expect it to work just fine for everybody, 
but give me a holler if you find any any window problems with it.

c) The list prints out the list of monsters killed in descending order of
Hit Dice, from Demogoron down to something like grid bug.  You should get a
listing like:
Asmodeus
:
The Wizard of Yendor (5 times)
:
1 kraken
:
The Oracle
:
43 hill orcs

Unique monsters will not have a number in front of them, and will only
be listed if you actually killed them.  I treat the Wizard of Yendor
and the oracle as two special case.  The Wiz is the same one, killed 
multiple times, so that's what I show.  The game calls the Orcle "oracle",
even though it is unique.  I thought it would be more dignified for
the Oracle to be called "The Oracle" on the list, rather than "oracle".
You can change it back if that is a problem...

Anyway, there's been far more than enough babbling by me - here it is, enjoy!

-Bryon Daly

